#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162849} :activity/id #uuid "22c8da45-56d1-5645-85df-aa94cccb29d8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049849 :topic/id #uuid "1887f465-a1b8-5370-82f6-80e02b9d153a" :topic/math-academy-id 3120} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162849 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163224 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $\\mathbb{R}.$ The **spectral theorem** states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- The eigenvectors corresponding to *different* eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable." :tutorial/id #uuid "5ecbbdd0-25a0-52ee-8f0c-987dcd8ba584" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4882 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "84b2f14e-f6c7-5b6c-b6d7-19d4d7d71f71" :step/math-academy-id 30533 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162902}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162902 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162013 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161805 :question/id #uuid "6ae71f41-133f-50b6-a286-20ae7e2c3a9d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8291"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c67d532-acd9-5231-a27f-d39084863e01" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048493}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366657} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478420}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Spectral Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "95f72e96-f84b-5b21-a070-253638be57a8" :step/math-academy-id 30970 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162903}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162903 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163243 :tutorial/content "We can write an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix $A$ in terms of a sum of products of its eigenvalues and eigenvectors.\n\nSpecifically, if $\\mathbf{u}_i$ denotes an orthonormal eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_i$ for $i=1,\\ldots, n$, then\n\n$$\nA=\\sum_{i=1}^n \\lambda_i \\mathbf{u}_i \\mathbf{u}_i^T.\n$$\n\nExpressing a symmetric matrix $A$ in this way is called the **spectral decomposition** of $A.$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the spectral decomposition of the $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/40/408e7471e4ef788758d423af97f5683186f79192fd8933c350d45bb87f089875.svg)\n\nThe eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1 = 6$ and $\\lambda_2 = -4,$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are\n\n![](images/0c/0c22f7dd97e83c358cb3cef1fcc2db902d908140109334a8cf1c22b35b29c6d2.svg)\n\nThe spectral decomposition of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA =\\sum_{i=1}^2 \\lambda_i \\mathbf{u}_i \\mathbf{u}_i^T = \\lambda_1 \\mathbf u_1 \\mathbf u_1^T + \\lambda_2 \\mathbf u_2 \\mathbf u_2^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u_1$ and $\\mathbf u_2$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spectral decomposition of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3\\end{aligned}]+(-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 3\\end{aligned}]+(-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{6}_{⏟}}_{{\\lambda }_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10} & \\frac{3}{10} \\\\ \\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{9}{10}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}}+{{(-4)}_{⏟}}_{{\\lambda }_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{10} & -\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this decomposition, the matrix $\\mathbf{u}_i \\mathbf{u}_i^T$ is the orthogonal projection matrix that maps any $\\mathbf x \\in \\mathbb R^2$ onto the subspace spanned by $\\mathbf u_i.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a90957e3-e4b9-548d-930b-e060c6b63859" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4973 :tutorial/title "The Spectral Decomposition of a Symmetric Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "8df507ec-4d53-59f1-b941-07907cd14b6f" :step/math-academy-id 30971 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162905}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162904 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162014 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161806 :question/id #uuid "d185ef42-c177-5217-99b7-b42d800f3385" :question/math-academy-id "e-8292"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84207049-12f7-514c-bfa2-51bce8631b41" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049489}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162646} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407552}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Projection Matrix That Orthogonally Projects a Vector to the Subspace Spanned by an Eigenvector"} :step/id #uuid "2a1d5d86-333c-5361-87ec-fd7766797988" :step/math-academy-id 30972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162905 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162015 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161807 :question/id #uuid "65b23c21-528f-5df7-a00c-8e2dce041276" :question/math-academy-id "e-8293"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "785b09aa-1b82-5021-8b10-271a063cb916" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407553}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Spectral Decomposition of a Symmetric Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "86248bdd-6a75-54cd-8196-8b333a64cac5" :step/math-academy-id 30973 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162904}}] :activity/title "The Spectral Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162216}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407555} :activity/id #uuid "cb802846-7e96-5e29-a7fd-43a85cc938d8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049849 :topic/id #uuid "1887f465-a1b8-5370-82f6-80e02b9d153a" :topic/math-academy-id 3120} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407554 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407550 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "237e9827-741e-554d-b4bd-a580c12832ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407524} {:answer/id #uuid "637ff16c-2a36-5623-9258-1f3130b80075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407529} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8f4850-6613-5ef7-a355-f12825aba902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407532} {:answer/id #uuid "bea7dec3-53c9-52fb-9b61-30210ef0b89a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407538} {:answer/id #uuid "d23ed5d0-4d06-561d-902d-1213f1013134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407539}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407524} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b7eee37-4841-5d7e-83f1-ad47a007de82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "69f0b9bd-9336-5c1c-91e3-bf72b33b1333" :question/math-academy-id "q-140847" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=8,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+8\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+8\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+8\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}10 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "07386288-c5f3-55df-b73a-99cec25cb7d6"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407555 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162646 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1556dc1b-0c8b-54e0-a340-18371f592ed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407520} {:answer/id #uuid "286d06a8-1050-59ed-a665-558d840c2aae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407525} {:answer/id #uuid "d6effcc8-e643-5b20-9090-b3855faacab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407541} {:answer/id #uuid "e9f4f75f-447a-5e0c-a1d6-feae985a30a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407543} {:answer/id #uuid "f93a6cf9-78b6-546d-ba88-361fc48a056a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407544}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407544} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebade115-5b0c-52d7-94cc-8ec9e5babad9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "11bdef24-ff68-513b-bfa6-5f2fb8c78b02" :question/math-academy-id "q-94768" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =-4.$ Computing $A-(-4)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+4I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}]+4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2d35b336-2654-53f7-8116-4689735d233e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407557}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407556 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407551 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16877fa0-d3a3-5da8-ba77-c7e71244a7c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407521} {:answer/id #uuid "6d264661-257d-5c8a-8684-122b5c0a6807" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407531} {:answer/id #uuid "9555f6b0-dd96-561e-b956-a28b10613e7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407535} {:answer/id #uuid "ad0b779d-af50-58f8-97aa-dd75027108dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407536} {:answer/id #uuid "d6231cbd-de79-555a-b056-b8008595ed25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407540}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407531} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1092e23-b9a3-5556-b0d1-0745f95be4b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7e3f233e-913d-50cf-8912-1c6832afca12" :question/math-academy-id "q-95725" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+(-9)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+(-9)\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}(4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+(-9)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}(4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{aligned}]+(-9)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}9 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}-65 & 78 \\\\ 78 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5fc71f89-cc9a-549d-9f1f-b3a2081e4a63" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407554}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407557 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407552 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d327dec-d42a-581c-8707-926d0b6893a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{17}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407522} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3ec327-8643-5dfe-8735-8c0774db03ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407526} {:answer/id #uuid "799755e8-7ecf-5b56-8460-da2805b858a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{17}[\\begin{aligned}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407533} {:answer/id #uuid "95054c16-a500-55cc-9c6d-5dc257b93966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407534} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b2cd88-0c1c-5067-96b8-f34a70eda38d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{17}[\\begin{aligned}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407542}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407542} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb85388f-97d4-5b7a-9384-bb45d3a9d42b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9d231abd-db3d-5d11-9253-820e7eb51742" :question/math-academy-id "q-95712" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 18\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =2.$ Computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 18\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 16\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 16 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=4{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{17}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}[\\begin{aligned}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "61132d09-e84e-51b9-b83a-aedca27c1563" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407558}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407558 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407553 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f090535-e10a-5c59-9a51-2d2aae1cbc9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407523} {:answer/id #uuid "3f43beb7-1a8e-5d63-bf78-07894e8c2c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407527} {:answer/id #uuid "5613d7c8-bedb-54d2-be91-7e80b596eb5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407528} {:answer/id #uuid "694df5b3-0ed3-5486-8f0d-63bf6d2b9e75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407530} {:answer/id #uuid "bc550768-52c7-5626-83cb-470760bd86c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407537}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407523} :answer-field/id #uuid "a49b921e-a620-50dc-993e-f6b1a37ca3f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b502656e-ef35-5f6c-921c-ae3a8ea1f2a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-95721" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-6,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}]+(-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}]+(-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}]+(-6)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]+(-6)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-25 & 10 \\\\ 10 & -10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "639ff62f-ac48-56c2-bf2b-580bf31ccf01" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407556}}] :activity/title "The Spectral Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407519}]}
